" (https://images.darkhorse.com/common/salestools/catalogs/DH_Manga_Guide.pdf)\nciteturn11view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 37; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://images.darkhorse.com/common/salestools/catalogs/DH_Manga_Guide.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1108\nL0@P0: DARK HORSE COMICS\nL1@P0: ce l e brates y ears o f ma ng a\nL2@P0: DARK HORSE COMICS\nL3@P0: ce l e brates y ears o f ma ng a\nL4@P0: A G U I D E T O\nL5@P0-1: DARK HORSE MANGALWCUB #1 12PR PG 2\u0018\u0018\nL6@P1: 2\u00183 Lone Wolf and Cub. See page 30.\nL7@P1: I ntrodu c tion\nL8@P1: Long before the term “manga” was used in the industry, and long before manga had become\nL9@P1: a buzzword, a trend, and a hot seller, Mike Richardson had one ideal for his company in\nL10@P1: mind: publish the best comics from around the globe, regardless of their country of\nL11@P1: origin. It was a rainy day in May, 1988, when Kazuhisa Iwata’s Godzilla advance copies\nL12@P1: arrived at the (then-small) Milwaukie office. Given the market dictum of the time, a\nL13@P1: cursory glance might have led one to believe that this was just another comic book—\nL14@P1: the term “manga format” didn’t exist, and the whole “flopped/unflopped” controversy\nL15@P1: (reading left to right versus right to left) wouldn’t exist for another fifteen or so years—\nL16@P1: but this comic book marked Dark Horse’s first step into the manga publishing world.\nL17@P1: It’s not shocking, given the current rush to publish manga, that just about every publishing\nL18@P1: company has a manga imprint, and over twelve manga-only publishing companies have\nL19@P1: sprung up since 2000. But great manga isn’t just a selling tool. Simply because a book\nL20@P1: is manga doesn’t necessarily mean that it will be a blockbuster bestseller. Here at Dark\nL21@P1: Horse, where we’ve been publishing manga for over twenty years, we appreciate that the\nL22@P1: strength of a property lies not in its genre or nationality, but in its overall excellence.\nL23@P1: And it seems that both professionals and fans agree! In their blog on activeanime.com,\nL24@P1: fellow manga publisher Del Rey included two Dark Horse titles—MPD-Psycho and\nL25@P1: Kurosagi Corpse Delivery Service—in their list of top-ten picks of the year. Publishers\nL26@P1: Weekly gave MPD-Psycho the number-two spot on their “Top Ten Manga of 2007” list.\nL27@P1: Shoujo-style standout Translucent was lauded by Pop Culture Shock, which praised\nL28@P1: that “sometimes humorous, sometimes bittersweet, this lovely coming-of-age story is a\nL29@P1: shoo-in for YALSA’s 2007 List of Great Graphic Novels for Teens.” Eden, an intracompany\nL30@P1: favorite, receives constant recognition, and indeed might be the most critically acclaimed\nL31@P1: series of 2007, with Wizard magazine naming it “The Best Manga of 2007,” Newtype\nL32@P1: magazine calling it “the best manga that American money can buy,” and Newsarama\nL33@P1: hailing it as “The Best It’s-New-To-Me Manga of 2007.” There are certainly many\nL34@P1: readers in the marketplace today who are discovering Dark Horse—or rediscovering, as\nL35@P1-2: the case may be—thanks to the strength of Dark Horse’s manga line.It would be hard to discuss Dark Horse’s role as a manga publisher without talking\nL36@P2: specifically about our long-standing involvement in publishing the best seinen (mature\nL37@P2: readers) manga series out there. As a company that has always championed creator\nL38@P2: rights, we’ve always endeavored to protect the original visions of the creators behind our\nL39@P2: manga titles, and have earned a strong reputation in the industry as a publisher that\nL40@P2: does not censor artwork or pull any other punches when bringing quality mature\u0002readers Japanese works to the English-speaking market. But we don’t just focus on\nL41@P2: seinen titles . . . Our manga line also offers top-notch humor, romance, fantasy, science\nL42@P2: fiction, and adventure titles for all ages!\nL43@P2: Most people have already discovered Dark Horse through our product lines, our projects\nL44@P2: themselves, and their multinational motion-picture counterparts. But for those who are\nL45@P2: new to us, we are proud to present this guide to Dark Horse manga as a way to introduce\nL46@P2: you to some of the amazing multicultural entertainment we have to offer.\nL47@P2: D e f inition o f T erms\nL48@P2: Anime—A general term for animated Japanese television shows and movies.\nL49@P2: Bunkobon—A smaller manga format, such as that in which Lone Wolf and Cub\nL50@P2: is produced.\nL51@P2: Flopped—A throwback to when manga was first introduced to the North\nL52@P2: American direct market. “Flopping” involves transposing Japanese comics pages so\nL53@P2: that instead of reading in the Japanese fashion—from right to left—they read in the\nL54@P2: left-to-right manner familiar to Western audiences.\nL55@P2: Josei—Manga intended for adult women.\nL56@P2: Kodomo—Manga intended for children.\nL57@P2: Manga—A general term for Japanese comics, sometimes used incorrectly to\nL58@P2: denote all Asian comics.\nL59@P2: Manhwa—Korean comics. Although often grouped into the “manga” category,\nL60@P2: these titles have a distinct culture and style that sets them apart from Japanese comics.\nL61@P2: Otaku—A self-imposed term for a die-hard manga fan.\nL62@P2: Seinen—Manga intended for adult men.\nL63@P2: Serialization—The process by which ongoing series are distributed in weekly\nL64@P2: or monthly installments before being collected in trade paperback (TPB) format.\nL65@P2: ShoUjo—Manga intended for young women/teens.\nL66@P2: ShoUnen—Manga intended for young men/teens.\nL67@P2: Tankobon—Today’s “standard” manga TPB format.\nL68@P2: Unflopped—The original format of Japanese comics pages, which reflects the\nL69@P2: Japanese right-to-left reading style. The current standard for publishing manga is\nL70@P2-3: to leave work unflopped.MPD-Psycho. See page 32.\nL71@P3: INDEX\nL72@P3: Appleseed8\nL73@P3: Astro Boy 10\nL74@P3: Berserk 12\nL75@P3: Blade of the Immortal 14\nL76@P3: Blood+ 16\nL77@P3: Eden 18\nL78@P3: Gantz 20\nL79@P3: Gunsmith Cats (revised omnibus editions) 22\nL80@P3: Gunsmith Cats: Burst 24\nL81@P3: Hellsing 26\nL82@P3: The Kurosagi Corpse Delivery Service 28\nL83@P3: Lone Wolf and Cub 30\nL84@P3: MPD-Psycho 32\nL85@P3: Oh My Goddess! 34\nL86@P3: Old Boy 36\nL87@P3: Orion 38\nL88@P3: Path of the Assassin 40\nL89@P3: Samurai Executioner 42\nL90@P3: Translucent 44\nL91@P3: Trigun 46\nL92@P3: Trigun Maximum 48 Other Notable & Important Japanese Works from Dark Horse\nL93@P3: The Art of Yoshitaka Amano 52\nL94@P3: Blood+ Illustrated Novels 56\nL95@P3: The Dayan Collection 58\nL96@P3: The Dirty Pair Illustrated Novels 60\nL97@P3: Gungrave Archives 62\nL98@P3: Oh My Goddess! First End Novel 64\nL99@P3: Roman Album: Samarai Champloo 66\nL100@P3: Style School 68\nL101@P3-4: Vampire Hunter D Illustrated Novels 708 9\nL102@P4: APPLESEED\nL103@P4: FAST FACTS\nL104@P4: Writer: Shirow Masamune\nL105@P4: Artist: Shirow Masamune\nL106@P4: Format: B&W (ID contains full-color sections),\nL107@P4: softcover, 5 ¾\" x 8 ¼\"\nL108@P4: Age: 14+\nL109@P4: Volumes: Appleseed vols 1–4, Appleseed: ID,\nL110@P4: Appleseed: Hypernotes\nL111@P4: Sales: Series sales over 115,000\nL112@P4: Note\nL113@P4: World War III is over, and nomad soldier Deunan\nL114@P4: Knute and her cyborg partner Briareos struggle to\nL115@P4: survive in the abandoned cities and demilitarized zones of a postwar wasteland, the Badside.\nL116@P4: Matters appear on the upswing, however, when they are found and brought to Olympus, an\nL117@P4: urban utopia and centerpiece for the reconstruction of civilization. Deunan and Bri join the\nL118@P4: Olympus police, a force that seems hardly necessary in such a paradise. But, like in most\nL119@P4: pretty pictures, perfection is an illusion, and Olympus’s peaceful facade hides a dark secret,\nL120@P4: a violent struggle between humans and cyborgs that could once again plunge the world into\nL121@P4: war . . . and genocide.\nL122@P4: Written and drawn by Shirow Masamune, creator of Ghost in the Shell—one of the highest\u0002grossing anime of all time—Appleseed has garnered wide acclaim for its masterful blending of\nL123@P4: high-octane action and science fiction with thought-provoking questions about philosophy,\nL124@P4: sociology, and politics. Shirow packs Appleseed volumes 1–4 with heavy-hitting, mecha\u0002battling, police-procedural tales, while in Appleseed: ID and Appleseed: Hypernotes he takes\nL125@P4: his readers on a guided tour of the people, places, organizations, and, of course, technology\nL126@P4: that make the universe of Appleseed the sci-fi hotspot that it is, as well as examining his own\nL127@P4: creative process. Several cinematic incarnations of the Appleseed franchise, including the\nL128@P4: most recent, Appleseed: Ex Machina, produced by John Woo, have given this property a\nL129@P4-5: continuing cultural relevance.10 11\nL130@P5: ASTRO BOY\nL131@P5: FAST FACTS\nL132@P5: Writer: Osamu Tezuka\nL133@P5: Artist: Osamu Tezuka\nL134@P5: Format: B&W, softcover, 4 ½\" x 6 ¾\"\nL135@P5: Age: 8+\nL136@P5: Volumes: 1–23\nL137@P5: Sales: Over 150,000 copies sold\nL138@P5: Note\nL139@P5: Dark Horse proudly presents one of the crown jewels\nL140@P5: of international graphic fiction—Astro Boy! Created by\nL141@P5: the late Osamu Tezuka, the “God of Manga,” a revered\nL142@P5: animator and cartoonist who created over 150,000\nL143@P5: pages of comics in his career and is considered the Walt Disney of Japan, Astro Boy was the\nL144@P5: first manga series to be adapted to animation. Astro Boy became a worldwide phenomenon,\nL145@P5: making Astro Boy the Mickey Mouse of anime and manga—a jet-powered, super-strong,\nL146@P5: evil-robot-bashing, alien-invasion-smashing Mickey Mouse, that is! Exciting, whimsical,\nL147@P5: and touching, Astro Boy hearkens back to the classic era of comics and animation, featuring\nL148@P5: stories that readers young and old will enjoy!\nL149@P5: An impressive cornerstone in the foundation of Japan’s “robot culture,” Astro Boy remains\nL150@P5: hugely popular today, with a live-action movie slated for release in North America in 2009!\nL151@P5: • Astroboy Volumes 1 & 2 are now available as a collected, value-priced omnibus edition\nL152@P5: to help get you into manga’s most beloved series right away!\nL153@P5: • There is a feature-length Astroboy motion picture slated for release in 2009!\nL154@P5-6: • Dark Horse’s most popular all-ages manga title!12 13\nL155@P6: BERSERK\nL156@P6: FAST FACTS\nL157@P6: Writer: Kentaro Miura\nL158@P6: Artist: Kentaro Miura\nL159@P6: Format: B&W, softcover, 5 ¹/ 8\" x 7 ¼\"\nL160@P6: Age: 18+\nL161@P6: Volumes: 1–28 (ongoing series)\nL162@P6: Sales: Over 500,000 copies sold\nL163@P6: NOTE\nL164@P6: His name is Guts, the Black Swordsman, a feared warrior\nL165@P6: spoken of only in whispers. Bearer of a gigantic sword,\nL166@P6: an iron hand, and the scars of countless battles and\nL167@P6: tortures, his flesh is also indelibly marked with the\nL168@P6: Brand, an unholy symbol that draws the forces of darkness to him and dooms him as their\nL169@P6: sacrifice. But Guts won’t take his fate lying down; he’ll cut a crimson swath of carnage through\nL170@P6: the ranks of the damned—and anyone else foolish enough to oppose him! Accompanied by\nL171@P6: Puck the Elf, more an annoyance than a companion, Guts relentlessly follows a dark, blood\u0002stained path that leads only to death . . . or vengeance.\nL172@P6: A mainstay in Japan, with over ten million copies sold, Berserk is Japanese publisher\nL173@P6: Hakusensha’s number-one seinen title and the wellspring for an internationally popular\nL174@P6: anime series. One of manga’s great epic action/fantasy sagas, Berserk can be likened to\nL175@P6: Western classics like Beowulf and Lord of the Rings in scope and scale. But Berserk also\nL176@P6: proudly embodies manga mayhem at its most extreme; violent, horrifying, and mercilessly\nL177@P6: funny, Berserk is not for the squeamish or the easily offended. Berserk asks for no quarter—\nL178@P6: and offers none!\nL179@P6: “Miura’s illustration captures the primal fear of the dark with forms that are chilling when\nL180@P6-7: masked in darkness and horrifying when revealed.” —Ain’t It Cool News14 15\nL181@P7: FAST FACTS\nL182@P7: Writer: Hiroaki Samura\nL183@P7: Artist: Hiroaki Samura\nL184@P7: Format: B&W, softcover, 5 3/ 8\" x 8 ¼\"\nL185@P7: Age: 16+\nL186@P7: Volumes: 1–20 (ongoing series)\nL187@P7: Sales: Over 450,000 copies sold\nL188@P7: NOTES\nL189@P7: Manji is one of the most skilled samurai in all of\nL190@P7: feudal Japan. He is also immortal, condemned to a\nL191@P7: life of combat until he spills the blood of a thousand\nL192@P7: evil men. But along with eternal life comes the need to make an eternal living, and so Manji\nL193@P7: works as bodyguard for Rin, who seeks to personally avenge her parents’ murders at the hands\nL194@P7: of one of Japan’s deadliest swordsmen, Anotsu Kagehisa. But even with Manji’s help and her\nL195@P7: own burning thirst for retribution, can Rin ever stand against such a powerful and ruthless\nL196@P7: adversary? And in her quest to kill Anotsu, does she risk becoming more like him?\nL197@P7: Winner of the coveted Eisner Award for Best U.S. Edition of Foreign Material in 2000, as\nL198@P7: well as countless other awards both in Japan and the U.S., Blade of the Immortal is a stunning\nL199@P7: reinvention of the classic samurai epic, a complex blending of historical drama, modern\nL200@P7: street idiom, personal horror, and the supernatural. Progenitor of genre-bending hits like\nL201@P7: Samurai Champloo, this series is a masterpiece of comics storytelling.\nL202@P7: • Dark Horse plans to release a collector’s-edition Blade of the Immortal art book and\nL203@P7: the first-ever Blade of the Immortal novel in 2010!\nL204@P7: BLADE O F THE\nL205@P7: IMMMORTAL\nL206@P7-8: *Please note, the facing page reads left-to-right. It has been flopped at the creator’s request.16 17\nL207@P8: FAST FACTS\nL208@P8: Writer: Asuka Katsura\nL209@P8: Artist: Asuka Katsura\nL210@P8: Format: B&W, softcover, 5 ¹/ 8\" x 7 ¼\"\nL211@P8: Age: 14+\nL212@P8: Volumes: 1–5\nL213@P8: Sales: First printing immediately sold out; over\nL214@P8: 18,000 copies sold in the first two weeks. Over\nL215@P8: 50,000 since first release in 2007.\nL216@P8: NOTE\nL217@P8: Set several decades after the events in the popular\nL218@P8: Blood: The Last Vampire anime movie, Blood+ follows\nL219@P8: the now-amnesiac Saya Otonashi, who lives as a seemingly normal high-school student with\nL220@P8: her adoptive family in Okinawa. Horrible nightmares are the only hints at the violent life she\nL221@P8: once led, but her past is about to catch up with her and awaken the merciless warrior within.\nL222@P8: Chiropterans—powerful shape-changing creatures who feast on blood—threaten humanity\nL223@P8: once more, and a mysterious organization called the Red Shield needs Saya’s deadly sword\nL224@P8: skills and mysterious powers to aid in the fight against these beasts. As her forgotten abilities\nL225@P8: begin to reawaken and she seeks to regain her memories, Chiropteran warriors guided by a\nL226@P8: mysterious new enemy threaten Saya and her loved ones.\nL227@P8: Asuka Katsura’s manga series successfully expands upon the original Production I.G/Aniplex\nL228@P8: feature, delivering moments of jarring violence and thrilling action in a tale that spans several\nL229@P8: centuries. Blood+ is a multimedia sensation, with an animated series now airing on Adult\nL230@P8: Swim. Dark Horse is also proud to present translated editions of the Blood+ novels! For\nL231@P8: more on these titles, please turn to page 56!\nL232@P8: “Blood+ the manga is an absorbing action horror series that shakes up the vampire mythos to offer a\nL233@P8: compelling journey of self-discovery and growing horror for one lost girl.” —Active Anime\nL234@P8-9: BLOOD +18 19\nL235@P9: FAST FACTS\nL236@P9: Writer: Hiroki Endo\nL237@P9: Artist: Hiroki Endo\nL238@P9: Format: B&W, softcover, 5 ¹/ 8\" x 7 ¼\"\nL239@P9: Age: 18+\nL240@P9: Volumes: 1–11 (ongoing series)\nL241@P9: Sales: Over 40,000 copies sold\nL242@P9: NOTE\nL243@P9: In the near future, a brutal new virus that hardens\nL244@P9: the skin while dissolving internal organs wipes out a\nL245@P9: huge portion of humanity. Those who aren’t immune\nL246@P9: are either severely crippled or only able to live thanks\nL247@P9: to cybernetically enhanced bodies. Taking advantage\nL248@P9: of a world mired in social, political, and economic chaos, a paramilitary force known as\nL249@P9: Propater topples the United Nations and seeks world domination. Elijah, a young survivor\nL250@P9: searching for his mother, travels toward the Andes Mountains with an artificially intelligent\nL251@P9: combat robot named Cherubim. When he is kidnapped by a group of supposed freedom\nL252@P9: fighters who have plans to use Cherubim to their own ends, a brutal and poignant coming\u0002of-age story begins as Elijah is forced to come to terms with the realities of the world he lives\nL253@P9: in and the truth about his own family’s place in it.\nL254@P9: Graphic and philosophical, Eden is a cyberpunk powerhouse, both a brilliant love song to\nL255@P9: the postapocalyptic survival genre and a deep exploration of man’s role in the natural order,\nL256@P9: in which endearing heroes face a constant struggle for survival and violent surprises wait\nL257@P9: around every corner.\nL258@P9: Hiroki Endo’s Eden receives constant critical recognition, and may be the most acclaimed\nL259@P9: manga series of 2007, with Wizard magazine naming it “The Best Manga of 2007,” Newtype\nL260@P9: magazine calling it “the best manga that American money can buy,” and Newsarama heralding\nL261@P9: it as “The Best It’s-New-To-Me Manga of 2007.”\nL262@P9-10: EDEN20 21\nL263@P10: FAST FACTS\nL264@P10: Writer: Hiroya Oku\nL265@P10: Artist: Hiroya Oku\nL266@P10: Format: B&W, softcover, 5 ¹/ 8\" x 7 ¼\"\nL267@P10: Age: 18+\nL268@P10: Volumes: 1–4 (ongoing series)\nL269@P10: Sales: Over 25,000 sold since release in 2008.\nL270@P10: NOTE\nL271@P10: The last thing Kei and Masaru remember was being\nL272@P10: struck dead by a subway train while saving the life of\nL273@P10: a drunken bum. What a waste! And yet somehow they’re still . . . alive? Or semi-alive? Perhaps\nL274@P10: reanimated by the alien orb that delivers them a nasty message: “Your lives are over. What\nL275@P10: you do with your new lives is up to me!” This orb, called “Gantz,” intends to make them\nL276@P10: play its twisted games of death, pitting them and a bunch of other ordinary citizens who’ve\nL277@P10: recently met a tragic semi-end against all kinds of odd alien creatures. The missions they\nL278@P10: embark upon are often dangerous. Many die—and die again, yet one agonizing question\nL279@P10: remains: how long must they stay in the game?\nL280@P10: Gantz has been one of the most popular adult manga series in recent memory, a dark and\nL281@P10: action-packed exploration of the moral conflicts of violence, teenage sexual confusion and\nL282@P10: angst, and our fascination with death. Running weekly in the Japanese magazine Young\nL283@P10: Jump, which has had a record circulation of over three million readers, Gantz has not only\nL284@P10: boasted impressive TPB sales numbers. It has also inspired a hit anime series, which has sold\nL285@P10: over 300,000 units!\nL286@P10: “Drawn with eye-popping realism, Oku’s scenes range from infanticide to bestiality to painful adolescent\nL287@P10: awkwardness, raising standard geek fare to a new level of sophistication.” —Entertainment Weekly\nL288@P10-11: GANTZ22 23\nL289@P11: FAST FACTS\nL290@P11: Writer: Kenichi Sonoda\nL291@P11: Artist: Kenichi Sonoda\nL292@P11: Format: B&W, softcover, 5 ¹/ 8\" x 7 ¼\"\nL293@P11: Age: 18+\nL294@P11: Volumes: 1–4\nL295@P11: Sales: Over 150,000 copies sold\nL296@P11: NOTE\nL297@P11: Rally Vincent and Minnie-May Hopkins are experts in\nL298@P11: their respective fields of marksmanship and explosives,\nL299@P11: but they’re so cute you’d never know it, and neither would the perps unlucky enough to be\nL300@P11: their prey! Whether they’re chasing bail jumpers or fending off the mob, you can bet on a\nL301@P11: healthy supply of guns, bombs, car chases, and more guns!\nL302@P11: Kenichi Sonoda’s sexy stories about these two bounty-hunting girls on the dangerous streets\nL303@P11: of Chicago have always been a favorite of manga fans. So when the new omnibus editions\nL304@P11: saw print in Japan, we knew they should find a home at Dark Horse, where we’ve been\nL305@P11: publishing Gunsmith Cats manga since 1995! These giant-sized, revised omnibus editions are\nL306@P11: action packed, unaltered, and sure to please gun nuts, auto buffs, and manga maniacs alike,\nL307@P11: and mark the first time these stories have been published unflopped in North America!\nL308@P11: “. . . Gunsmith Cats is a superb series worthy of just about anyone’s collection.” —The A.V. Club\nL309@P11: GUNSMITH C ATS\nL310@P11-12: (revised omnibus editionS)24 25\nL311@P12: FAST FACTS\nL312@P12: Writer: Kenichi Sonoda\nL313@P12: Artist: Kenichi Sonoda\nL314@P12: Format: B&W, softcover, 5 ¹/ 8\" x 7 ¼\"\nL315@P12: Age: 18+\nL316@P12: Volumes: 1–3 (ongoing series)\nL317@P12: Sales: Over 25,000 copies sold\nL318@P12: NOTE\nL319@P12: Gunsmith Cats: Burst finds Rally Vincent and Minnie\u0002May Hopkins, the gun-slinging, bomb-wielding young\nL320@P12: bounty hunters made famous in Kenichi Sonoda’s\nL321@P12: hit manga series Gunsmith Cats, back at work, back\nL322@P12: in action, and back in trouble!\nL323@P12: “. . . it’s great to see a combination of detailed\nL324@P12: machinery and cute anime faces. Throw in some\nL325@P12: guns and some fantastic action sequences and\nL326@P12: Dark Horse have themselves a winner.”\nL327@P12:  —About heroes\nL328@P12: GUNSMITH C ATS:\nL329@P12-13: BURST26 27\nL330@P13: FAST FACTS\nL331@P13: Writer: Kohta Hirano\nL332@P13: Artist: Kohta Hirano\nL333@P13: Format: B&W, softcover, 5 ¹/ 8\" x 7 ¼\"\nL334@P13: Age: 16+\nL335@P13: Volumes: 1–9 (ongoing series)\nL336@P13: Sales: Over 625,000 copies sold\nL337@P13: NOTE\nL338@P13: The Hellsing Organization, a secret agency charged\nL339@P13: with defending Queen and country from all manner\nL340@P13: of supernatural threats, wages a clandestine war on\nL341@P13: any vampires, ghouls, and other monsters foolish\nL342@P13: enough to stalk the British Isles. They’ve got all the experience, know-how, and . . . er . . .\nL343@P13: “equipment” necessary to handle all the things that go bump in the night. What “equipment,”\nL344@P13: you ask? How about an ancient vampire wielding a big pistol loaded with special silver bullets?\nL345@P13: That oughta do the trick! But with a world war of the undead looming on the horizon, does\nL346@P13: the Hellsing Organization have what it takes to avert an apocalypse heralded by the march\nL347@P13: of a horde of Nazi vampires and their ghoulish legions?\nL348@P13: Earth-shaking destruction and gore-soaked supernatural battles fill the pages of this dark\nL349@P13: and creepy-cool manga. The inspiration behind two mainstream hit anime series, Hellsing\nL350@P13: and Hellsing: Ultimate, this manga title never ceases to draw new readership and satisfy all\nL351@P13: of its die-hard fans.\nL352@P13: “The characters are magnificent to behold in action when they get into a frenzy, from crushing\nL353@P13: heads beneath their boot heels to impaling an enemy with an untold number of blessed blades.\nL354@P13: Needless to say, Hirano does violence right.” —Animerica\nL355@P13-14: HELLSING28 29\nL356@P14: FAST FACTS\nL357@P14: Writer: Eiji Otsuka\nL358@P14: Artist: Housui Yamazaki\nL359@P14: Format: B&W, softcover, 5 ¹/ 8\" x 7 ¼\"\nL360@P14: Age: 18+\nL361@P14: Volumes: 1–8 (ongoing series)\nL362@P14: Sales: Over 30,000 copies sold\nL363@P14: NOTE\nL364@P14: Your body is their business! Five young students at a\nL365@P14: Buddhist university find little call for their job skills\nL366@P14: in today’s Tokyo . . . among the living, anyway. But\nL367@P14: all that stuff in college they were told would never\nL368@P14: pay off—you know, channeling, dowsing, ESP—gives them a direct line to the dead . . . the\nL369@P14: dead who are still trapped in their corpses and can’t move on to their next reincarnation\nL370@P14: without a little help! And so the enterprising five form the Kurosagi Corpse Delivery Service.\nL371@P14: Whether suicide, murder, accident, or illness is to blame, they’ll carry your body wherever\nL372@P14: it needs to go to free your soul! The kids from Kurosagi can smell a customer a mile away—\nL373@P14: it’s a good thing one of the girls majored in embalming!\nL374@P14: “While there is cruelty, gore, violence, and implied unnatural sexual acts, the manga is also a\nL375@P14: complex and slyly ironic social commentary. Acquire this manga for adult collections for the\nL376@P14: naturalistic artwork that shows attention to human emotion, the social observation, and the\nL377@P14: banter between members of the delivery service.” —Library Journal\nL378@P14: “Nobody does horror-comedy comics better than Otsuka and Yamazaki.” —Booklist\nL379@P14: KUROSAGI C ORPSE\nL380@P14-15: DELI V ERY SERV I C ELWCUB #1 12PR PG 21\nL381@P15: 21\nL382@P15: 30 31\nL383@P15: FAST FACTS\nL384@P15: Writer: Kazuo Koike\nL385@P15: Artist: Goseki Kojima\nL386@P15: Format: B&W, softcover, 4\" x 6\"\nL387@P15: Age: 16+\nL388@P15: Volumes: 1–28\nL389@P15: Sales: Over 1,000,000 copies sold, making LW&C\nL390@P15: Dark Horse’s best-ever-selling manga title!\nL391@P15: NOTE\nL392@P15: Ogami Itto, the shogun’s highly respected executioner,\nL393@P15: is forced to flee his former life and toil in exile after the\nL394@P15: cruel betrayal of his family by a fellow clan. Taking\nL395@P15: with him his infant son, Daigoro, Itto must survive as an assassin for hire, finding what work\nL396@P15: he can along the road as he sets out on a quest for vengeance and redemption. Through\nL397@P15: unimaginable violence, across hundreds of miles of blood-soaked roadbeds, over years of\nL398@P15: tragedy and anguish, Itto and Daigoro undertake a cursed journey in a world where honor is\nL399@P15: all but forgotten and warriors are obsolete. It’s a bloody battle all the way to the finish, with\nL400@P15: dramatic twists and turns right up to the final page!\nL401@P15: An epic samurai adventure of staggering proportions—weighing in at over seven thousand\nL402@P15: pages!—Lone Wolf and Cub has long been acknowledged worldwide as one of the single\n"
